    source:{ name:"UCLA", handle:"UCLA", platform:"web", kind:"news" },
    text:"UCLA researchers built an optical-neural processor that uses light rather than digital hardware to screen for deepfake video, analyzing 15+ video streams in a single optical pass. On the Celeb-DF dataset it hit roughly 97.8% average detection accuracy, 99.9% sensitivity, and 95.7% specificity, and the team reports it stays robust against compression, noise, and black-box adversarial attacks designed to fool it.",
    why:"Most deepfake-detection coverage this year has been about a tool failing or getting gamed; a result this clean, with adversarial-robustness testing included, is worth tracking for whether it reaches a deployable product rather than staying a lab result.",
